setl(271, f"In GSE25066, the 5th-percentile ESR1 threshold among IHC ER-positive tumours was 9.34 log2 units. Among the 205 IHC ER-negative tumours, 39 were called HEA (19.0%, 95% CI 14.2–24.9; Table 6). The thresholds rest on 297 reference tumours and are imprecise: when that reference was resampled, the ESR1 threshold was {gb['ESR1'][0]:.2f}–{gb['ESR1'][1]:.2f} and the HEA count {int(gb['HEA_count_fixed_negatives'][0])}–{int(round(gb['HEA_count_fixed_negatives'][1]))} (95% intervals; Table 4). The three decision rules of amendment A4 are reported below as they were written, together with the analyses of amendment A5.")
setl(273, f"- **Prevalence: higher than in SCAN-B.** HEA prevalence was 12.4 percentage points higher than in the SCAN-B HER2-negative stratum (95% CI 5.5–19.0) and 10.7 points higher than in all SCAN-B IHC ER-negative tumours (4.4–17.3). By the rule of amendment A4 it was not consistent with SCAN-B. The difference remained on a common universe of {R4['common_universe']} genes ({R4['common_signature_genes']} signature genes) with the SCAN-B thresholds taken from HER2-negative IHC ER-positive tumours (n = {R4['scanb_reference_HER2neg_ERpos']}): HEA was called in {b5['scanb'][0]} of {b5['scanb'][1]} SCAN-B tumours ({f1(b5['scanb'][2])}%, {f1(b5['scanb'][3])}–{f1(b5['scanb'][4])}) and in {b5['gse25066'][0]} of {b5['gse25066'][1]} GSE25066 tumours ({f1(b5['gse25066'][2])}%, {f1(b5['gse25066'][3])}–{f1(b5['gse25066'][4])}), a difference of {d(b5)}. It was also present at the 1st percentile ({d2(b1)}) and the 10th ({d2(b10)}). The common-universe call agreed with the original call at κ {R4['kappa_common_vs_original_call']['scanb']:.2f} in SCAN-B and {R4['kappa_common_vs_original_call']['gse25066']:.2f} in GSE25066. The activity-without-ESR1 group was also larger in GSE25066 (37.6% against 24.1%), and the concordant ER-negative group was smaller (37.1% against 66.4%).")
setl(274, f"- **Dependence on definition: present in both cohorts.** Between the 2.5th and 10th percentiles the HEA count fell from {kn(gc_['2.5'])} to {kn(gc_['10'])}, a {fold_g:.1f}-fold range; at the 1st percentile it was {kn(gc_['1'])} (Table 4). The threefold rule of amendment A4 was met, but that rule was arbitrary. The single-gene ESR1 call deposited with the series classified 25 IHC ER-negative tumours (12.2%) as ESR1-positive; its agreement with the primary HEA call was κ 0.67. As in SCAN-B, the alternative activity scores changed the count little (39–43), and the threshold percentile changed it most.")
setl(275, f"- **Composition: partly similar.** The first condition of amendment A4 was met: 20 of the 39 HEA tumours were PAM50 luminal (10) or Normal-like (10), against 3 of 76 concordant ER-negative tumours (none luminal, 3 Normal-like). However, 16 HEA tumours (41%) were Basal-like, against 2 of 20 in SCAN-B. The second condition was not met: AR expression was not clearly higher in the activity-without-ESR1 group than in the concordant ER-negative group (medians 7.85 and 7.73 log2 units; rank-biserial correlation 0.14; p = 0.13), and FOXA1 gave a similar result (p = 0.19). Only 13 of 77 tumours in this group were PAM50 HER2-enriched, against 38 of 58 in SCAN-B. By the rule, composition was not replicated in full. ESR1 and the activity score were correlated among IHC ER-negative tumours here too (ρ {rg['er_negative']['rho']:.2f}, {rg['er_negative']['ci'][0]:.2f}–{rg['er_negative']['ci'][1]:.2f}).")
