# ---- Discussion
setl(347, f"In a population-based cohort of 241 IHC ER-negative breast cancers, a transcriptomic hidden-ER-activity label was carried by about 1 in 12 tumours. The two measurements behind the label were highly repeatable on re-sequencing. The label was much less stable. Its count changed {fold_s:.1f}-fold between the 2.5th and 10th percentile thresholds, and in an external microarray cohort it was more than twice as common (19%). The tumours it captured were mixed: tumours with a luminal expression profile despite a negative IHC result, and samples with a normal-breast-like profile. A neighbouring group showed oestrogen-response gene expression without ESR1, together with high AR and FOXA1 expression in SCAN-B. No outcome, protein-level or functional data were available to validate the label.\n\nThis study adds an audit to earlier work, not a new biological finding. Brueffer et al. showed that RNA-sequencing classifiers reproduce IHC ER status in SCAN-B and that discordant cases exist in both directions [@21]. Kimbung et al. described the transcriptional heterogeneity of ER-low and ER-zero tumours in a larger SCAN-B series [@13]. Neither study examined the stability of the IHC-negative, RNA-positive label itself. The present analysis establishes four things about that label: the repeatability of its two measurements in replicate libraries; the size of the change in its count across thresholds and across constructs; the uncertainty contributed by estimating the thresholds; and its dependence on the cohort under a cohort-relative procedure. All four are descriptive. The findings on composition are associations in bulk expression data and are stated as hypotheses.")
setl(349, f"The central finding is that the repeatability of the measurements and the stability of the classification differ. The intraclass correlations of 0.99 or above, including across platforms, show that the RNA-seq measurements themselves are not the problem. This agrees with other reproducibility studies. PAM50 calls agreed in 90% of technical replicates [@25], and mRNA-based ESR1 testing was concordant across five European laboratories [@16]. Both studies located the residual disagreement elsewhere: in spatial heterogeneity within the tumour [@25] and in results close to assay cut-offs [@16]. The evidence for the HEA calls themselves is weaker than the evidence for the measurements. The joint call agreed in 135 of 136 replicate pairs, but the pairs that were high on both axes were almost all IHC ER-positive tumours, and no IHC ER-negative HEA tumour was sequenced twice. For the 20 HEA calls there is only a simulation, which assumes Gaussian technical error and leaves out sampling within the tumour. Under it, {int(c7['robust_lt10'][0])}–{int(c7['robust_lt10'][1])} of the 20 calls were stable once the uncertainty in the thresholds and in the noise estimates was included. Disagreement arose where the line is drawn: in a post hoc check, every replicate disagreement on the activity call occurred within two technical SDs of its threshold. Measurement noise was larger at low ESR1 expression, but allowing for this left the HEA calls as robust as before; it mainly added uncertainty for tumours with low ESR1 expression that were not called HEA. Sampling adds a further layer that a single specimen cannot capture: in SCAN-B, almost half of tumours called ER-negative by gene expression on the core biopsy were ER-positive on the surgical specimen [@26].\n\nThe threshold was the largest single influence on the count. The 5th percentile was fixed in advance, but it is arbitrary. Between the 2.5th and 10th percentiles the count varied {fold_s:.1f}-fold in SCAN-B and {fold_g:.1f}-fold in GSE25066. At the 1st percentile of IHC ER-positive tumours the call is not interpretable, because the IHC ER-positive group itself contains tumours with low ESR1, as the Basal-like ER-positive SCAN-B tumours illustrate [@24]. The thresholds are also estimates, and the usual confidence interval for prevalence ignores this; with the reference resampled, the interval widened to {f1(tb['prevalence_combined_interval'][0])}–{f1(tb['prevalence_combined_interval'][1])}% in SCAN-B and {f1(gb['prevalence_combined_interval'][0])}–{f1(gb['prevalence_combined_interval'][1])}% in GSE25066. A joint definition has two cut-offs. Tools built to explore two-dimensional cut-offs for pairs of genomic predictors show how strongly subgroup membership can depend on both [@Cheng], and work on data-driven cut-off selection shows that a chosen cut-off needs to be evaluated in separate data [@Majd]. Differences between constructs are a separate matter. The two classifiers deposited with GSE96058 were built to predict IHC ER status, and they agree with each other only at κ 0.27 among IHC ER-negative tumours. Any study reporting \"ER-positive by RNA, ER-negative by IHC\" tumours should therefore report the threshold, its anchor and the sensitivity of the count to both. Anchoring RNA thresholds to IHC, as we did, is the approach also taken by pan-tumour RNA-seq biomarker studies [@15].")
setl(351, body[350] + " Their ER-zero group was defined from the staining percentage, which the public deposit does not provide.")
setl(353, f"The external cohort gave a higher figure. With the same definition and thresholds re-anchored to its own IHC ER-positive tumours, 19.0% of IHC ER-negative tumours in GSE25066 were called HEA, against 6.7% of HER2-negative tumours in SCAN-B. The difference remained on a common gene set with matched reference groups ({f1(b5['gse25066'][2])}% against {f1(b5['scanb'][2])}%). We did not predict this. The comparison has limits. Thresholds were re-estimated in each cohort, so the analysis tests a cohort-relative procedure and not a fixed classifier, which the change of platform does not allow. The GSE25066 thresholds rest on 297 reference tumours and are imprecise. The two cohorts may also not define \"IHC ER-negative\" in the same way. Swedish practice uses a 10% cut-off [@24], and the cut-off behind the deposited SCAN-B field is not stated [@21], so the SCAN-B group may include ER-low tumours. The GSE25066 source study treated any nuclear ER immunostaining as grounds for endocrine therapy [@47], although its deposited ER field is binary and its exact definition is not given. A lower cut-off in GSE25066 would be expected to leave fewer ER-expressing tumours in its ER-negative group, not more, so this difference does not explain the excess; it does mean that the two groups are not the same population. GSE25066 is also a multi-centre neoadjuvant series of pre-treatment fine-needle aspirates and core biopsies, which contain less tissue than the surgical specimens of a population-based cohort, and its ESR1 values come from a single microarray probe set. The data cannot separate these explanations. The external validation is also asymmetric. GSE25066 has no technical replicates and no processing variables, so it could test prevalence, dependence on definition and composition, but not the repeatability of the measurements or the model of Section 3.5. Whatever the cause, the practical conclusion is the same as for the threshold: a prevalence of \"hidden ER activity\" is a property of the definition, the platform and the cohort together, and a single figure should not be carried from one setting to another. The figure is of the same order as the 16% overall subtype discordance between transcriptomic classification and IHC in the Copenhagen cohort [@20]. It is likewise consistent with the moderate agreement between IHC surrogates and PAM50 in other Swedish cohorts [@19], given that single-marker ER concordance between IHC and expression can exceed 95% [@16,@18].")
setl(355, f"The composition of the HEA group suggests hypotheses, not mechanisms. About half of the group consisted of luminal A/B tumours whose ESR1 expression lay in the middle of the IHC ER-positive range. These could reflect false-negative staining, ER-low tumours recorded as negative under a 10% cut-off, sampling of an ER-heterogeneous tumour [@25,@34] or registry error, and the deposited data cannot distinguish them. In Copenhagen, subtype–IHC discordance was prognostically relevant among IHC-luminal patients [@20], so discordant tumours cannot simply be assumed to be noise, even if they are few. A further third were PAM50 Normal-like, sat at the ESR1 threshold and had a higher normal/adipose proxy. This pattern is compatible with admixed normal breast tissue, but neither the Normal-like label, which is itself contested [@36], nor an adipose-gene proxy identifies the cellular source of the ESR1 signal. Adjustment for stromal and immune scores did not remove these calls, and that adjustment is limited. The scores were used as continuous covariates and not as purity estimates, because the ESTIMATE purity formula was calibrated on another platform; they index stroma and immune cells and say nothing about normal epithelium, which deconvolution methods tend to misassign as cancer epithelium [@35]. Locating the signal would need a breast-specific purity estimator [@37], or single-cell-informed and spatial methods that assign expression to tissue compartments [@Dong]. Finally, the larger \"activity without ESR1\" group was dominated in SCAN-B by HER2-enriched tumours with high AR and FOXA1 expression, which resembles the LAR/molecular apocrine entity [@27,@29,@31]. That entity is common enough to matter: about 11% of a neoadjuvant trial population [@30], and approximated by AR immunostaining [@28]. The link is an association. AR activity was not measured, Hallmark oestrogen-response expression does not by itself establish ER signalling, and AR acts differently in ER-negative than in ER-positive disease [@32]; the same scores are informative in ER-positive tumours [@33]. The association was not seen in GSE25066, a cohort selected for HER2-negative disease in which half of the HEA tumours were again luminal or Normal-like but two-fifths were Basal-like. The two axes of the definition are also correlated (ρ {rs['er_negative']['rho']:.2f} among IHC ER-negative tumours), so \"ESR1 high and activity high\" is less than two independent confirmations. The composition of these groups therefore varies between cohorts, and each of these explanations remains a hypothesis.")
setl(357, f"The model planned as a technical-only negative control needs a more careful reading than we gave it in the submitted version. It predicted the HEA call above chance (AUC {auc('HEA','all')}), and this persisted after composition adjustment and after removing Normal-like tumours. However, the prediction came from three summaries computed from the expression matrix itself. Processing metadata alone predicted the call only weakly ({auc('HEA','metadata_only')}), and platform did not. Mean expression, gene-detection rate and ribosomal-protein expression can carry tumour biology and cellular composition as well as RNA quality. The result shows that the call is associated with global properties of the expression profile. It does not show a technical cause, and our earlier statement that it placed an artefact on the ESR1 axis is withdrawn. We still recommend that studies of transcriptomic reclassification report such controls, with processing variables kept separate from expression-derived ones.")
dele(359, 360)
LIM = f"""- **No HEA tumour was measured twice.** The repeatability results concern the two measurements across their range and calls in replicate pairs that were almost all IHC ER-positive. The stability of the 20 HEA calls is a simulation result.
- **No functional or protein-level validation.** The study is entirely computational. HEA was not tested against quantitative ER protein, ER binding to chromatin, or the response to oestrogen or anti-oestrogens, and it is not evidence of functional ERα activity. Functional work in cell models, tumour explants or patient-derived organoids chosen for IHC-negative, RNA-positive status is the necessary next step.
- **ER cut-off and no ER percentage.** ER-zero and ER-low tumours could not be separated in either cohort, the cut-off behind the SCAN-B field is not stated, and the two cohorts may use different cut-offs. This matters because survival and molecular features change across the whole 1–59% range [@7], and because ER-low tumours could account for part of the apparent discordance.
- **External cohort.** The external cohort was added post hoc and differs from SCAN-B in platform, population, specimen type and IHC practice. Thresholds were re-estimated within it. It has no replicate pairs or processing variables, so the repeatability analysis and the model of Section 3.5 were tested in SCAN-B only. Its difference in prevalence cannot be assigned to any one of these factors.
- **Composition adjustment.** Stromal and immune scores were used as continuous covariates, not as purity estimates, and normal-epithelial content could not be measured. The description of the HEA group as a mixture rests on PAM50 labels and an adipose-gene proxy, not on a measurement of cell types.
- **Missing IHC ER status.** The 200 tumours without an IHC ER result resembled ER-negative tumours by PAM50 subtype, so missingness was not random and the analysed ER-negative set is probably incomplete. Their true ER status is unknown and could differ between tumours above and below the thresholds. Under extreme assumptions the prevalence would lie between {f1(R3['lower_bound'][2])}% and {f1(R3['upper_bound'][2])}%, so we cannot say that their exclusion had little effect.
- **No outcome inference.** Endocrine therapy was given to only 11 IHC ER-negative patients and to one HEA patient, with no deaths in the HEA group, so no predictive or prognostic inference is possible.
- **Analysis plan not publicly registered.** The plan and amendments A1–A4 were hashed internally. Their hashes, timestamps and the deviations log are released with the code, but their timing cannot be verified independently, so the distinction between planned and post hoc analyses rests on the authors' record. The analyses of amendments A2–A5 were added after the main results were known. Amendment A5 was made public before its analyses were run.
- **Exploratory background analyses.** The microRNA and cell-line analyses in Supplementary Note S1 do not test HEA tumours and are limited by small numbers and by the data types available."""
setl(363, LIM + "\n"); dele(364, 372)
setl(373, "These results bear on how such an assay result should be interpreted. They do not bear on how patients should be treated, because the study has no outcome data. The question is not academic: endocrine therapy may benefit some ER-low patients [@5,@6], and gene-expression ER status predicted tamoxifen benefit as well as IHC in a randomised trial [@17]. Both observations, however, concern tumours with measurable ER protein or clearly bimodal ESR1 expression. They do not extend to IHC ER-negative tumours labelled ER-active only by a transcriptomic score. Studies that propose such a label as a biomarker should establish that the definition is stable, show that it transfers between cohorts and platforms, report controls with processing variables kept separate from expression-derived ones, account for sample composition, and check whether the \"ER activity\" could be AR-related before claiming a clinically relevant subgroup. If such a subgroup is proposed, it will need prospective evaluation; adaptive designs that allow for uncertainty about a biomarker-defined subgroup are one option [@Zhang]. A discordant RNA result is a reason to re-examine the IHC, for example by repeat staining on a different block and quantitative reporting, as reviews of ER-low disease also recommend [@2,@3,@4]. For populations such as those in the Middle East and North Africa, where breast cancer often presents younger and at a more advanced stage [@44], cohorts with RNA, quantitative IHC and treatment data are needed to test whether an endocrine-responsive subset exists. Existing regional data on IHC–expression concordance and ESR1 methylation [@18,@46] are small.")
setl(377, "In IHC ER-negative breast cancer, a transcriptomic hidden-ER-activity label was carried by about 8% of tumours in a population-based RNA-sequencing cohort and by 19% in an external microarray cohort. The two measurements behind the label were highly repeatable, but the label depended on the threshold, the definition and the cohort, and the tumours it captured were heterogeneous. Whether individual HEA calls are stable was estimated by simulation only. The label is a descriptive transcriptomic finding. It should not be read as evidence of functional ER signalling or of endocrine sensitivity, neither of which this study examined. Its use as a biomarker would require quantitative IHC, functional validation, outcome data and evidence that a fixed definition transfers between cohorts.")
setl(381, "Figure S1: Technical replicate agreement for ESR1 and the ER-activity score (136 replicate pairs, 36 of them cross-platform); Figure S2: Paired tumour-minus-normal differences for five ESR1-targeting microRNAs in two TNBC datasets; Figure S3: Chromatin accessibility and expression at the ESR1 and AR loci in eight TNBC cell lines; Table S1: Post hoc checks requested during co-author review (amendments A2 and A3); Table S2: Analyses requested in peer review (amendment A5); Table S3: Datasets used for exploratory background; Table S4: ESR1-targeting microRNAs, tumour versus paired normal tissue; Table S5: Expression and accessibility in eight TNBC cell lines; Supplementary Note S1: Exploratory background on mechanisms of ER silencing (methods, results and references).")
